7006048560143,0.008533946238458157,-0.09610345959663391,0.011687611229717731,-0.015930185094475746,-0.05133475735783577,-0.013323428109288216,0.051223449409008026,0.03437964990735054,0.05839911475777626,0.03056732751429081,-0.03901885077357292,0.030466021969914436,-0.040987562388181686,-0.02704579196870327,0.08853697031736374,-0.01040730718523264,0.010336121544241905,0.013424287550151348,0.016741450875997543],"tokens":215},"length":929,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[13,28]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md","embedding":{},"length":114,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8],"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-05: :\n⨑ MOOD::1\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::✅]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::]\n[Nutrients and Water::✅]"},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md#\\Tuesday, March 05, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md#\\Tuesday, March 05, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-05.md#\\Tuesday, March 05, 2024","embedding":{},"length":941,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Tuesday, March 05, 2024","lines":[9,55],"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-05: \\Tuesday, March 05, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n- Playing through Zelda the tears of the kingdom\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [ ]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n- #til2024 Calibri and the new Microsoft's default font\n\t- Since 2007, Calibri is MS's default font. It was replaced by Aptos today\n\t\t- It replaced Times New Roman\n\t- There were 5 original font candidates and Aptos won out\n\t\t- The creator Steve Matteson wanted the font to have the universal appeal of the late NPR newscaster Carl Kassell and the astute tone of the Late Show host Stephen Colbert\n\t- It is a sans-serif with a bit more human touch\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.cday = date(\"2024-03-05\") SORT file.ctime asc\n\n```\n\n### Notes last touched today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"202"},"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Science is a strong-link problem: 📰 Summary (use your own words):\nThe author frames all problems into strong-linked or weak-linked and argues that we are treating science like a weak-linked problem when it shouldn't be. Because the best science drives progress and weak science doesn't matter in the end. So we shouldn't create barriers and make it hard for risky or cutting edge science to get funded.","length":337,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📰 Summary (use your own words)","lines":[9,11]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Science is a strong-link problem.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Science is a strong-link problem: ✍️ Notes:\n- Strong-link problems\n\t- problems where the overall quality depend on how good the **best stuff is**\n\t- music is a strong-link problem\n\t- olympics is a strong-link problem\n\t- friendship is a strong-link problem\n\t- investment is a strong-link problem\n\t- *it is harder to spot than weak-link problems*\n- Weak-link problems\n\t- problems where the overall quality depend on how good the *weak stuff* is\n\t- FDA focuses on the minimizing the health issue from problematic food\n\t- engine is a weak-link problem\n- Solving strong-link problem\n\t- increase outliers\n\t- don't gatekeep\n\t- ignore the worst\n\t- improve the best\n\t- accept risk since downside does not matter\n\t- *inverse for weak-link problem*\n- Science is a strong-link problem, and we are still in the dark-ages just less dark than before\n\t- best stuff is all that matters\n\t- people treat science as a weak-link problem\n\t- spend 15,000 collective year effort to prevent bad r","length":927,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[12,47]},"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > MLOps is mostly data engineering: :\n---\nsource:\n  - https://www.cpard.xyz/posts/mlops_is_mostly_data_engineering/\nauthor:\n  - foobar\ntags:\n  - machine-learning\n  - programming\n---","length":143,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > MLOps is mostly data engineering: 📰 Summary (use your own words):\nThere is an infrastructure need to run and operate machine learning in production. The category of jobs related to this is labelled as MLOps or \"Machine Learning Operations\", but it is becoming apparent that there is a lot of overlap with the traditional data engineering roles.\n\nThe landscape right now is littered with tools, and lots of the new ML focused tools are just duplications of what existed.","length":404,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📰 Summary (use your own words)","lines":[9,13]},"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/MLOps is mostly data engineering.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > MLOps is mostly data engineering: ✍️ Notes:\n* MLOps Stack\n\t* Deployment and serving of models\n\t\t* OctoML\n\t* Model quality and monitoring\n\t\t* Weights and biases\n\t* Model training\n\t\t* AWS Sagemaker\n\t* Feature stores\n\t\t* Tecton\n* There is nothing *unique* about the MLOps stack compared to the traditional data engineering and software engineering stack.\n* Deployment and Serving\n\t* We need to wrap the model in a clean API that can be exposed to the users. Enable scalable deployment of this service and operate in a needs based way. Continuous integration of new features without disrupting usage. Monitoring for problems and logging for debugging.\n\t* We don't actually need a new suite of tools for this, but rather enrich the existing platforms for ML models as the foundational software artifacts*. So then the ML Engineers can package their work easily.\n* Model Quality and Monitoring\n\t* ML challenges the existing platforms by adding an additional layer of complexity","length":927,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[14,38]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Notes on Web Scraping.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Notes on Web Scraping.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Notes on Web Scraping.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Notes on Web Scraping: :\n#programming\n\nSeparate web crawling and web scraping.\n\nUse something like Urlbox to save the page to S3, get a webhook when ready to scrape and use something to scrape\n\nPlaywright seems to be useful tool in the web scraping stack\n\nPandas `read_html()` seems useful","length":264,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Moores Law for Everything.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Moores Law for Everything.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Moores Law for Everything.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Moores Law for Everything: ✍️ Notes:\n- The recursive loop of innovation\n\t- Create phenomenal wealth, price of labor that the technology replaced will fall to 0\n\t- World change so rapidly and drastically that an equally drastic change in policy will be needed to distribute wealth\n\t- Improve standard of living for people more than ever\n- We are at the start of this tectonic shift and can pivot into the direction we wish to head towards\n- AI revolution\n\t- The coming change will center around our abilities to think, create, understand and reason\n\t- Progress made will be exponential\n- Moore's law for everything\n\t- Two ways to afford a good life, increase wealth or decrease price\n\t- To effectively increase society wealth, the best way is to decrease the cost of goods\n\t- We will discover new jobs - we always do after a technology revolution\n\t> #opinion Just because historically this has always happened doesn't mean it will hold if we don't know the root cause\n- Ca","length":934,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[13,43]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#📖 Short Summary (1 takeaway)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#📖 Short Summary (1 takeaway)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#📖 Short Summary (1 takeaway)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Greenlight: 📖 Short Summary (1 takeaway):\n- Make choices that gives your life the most amount of greenlights","length":67,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📖 Short Summary (1 takeaway)","lines":[7,9]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#🙊 Great quotes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#🙊 Great quotes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Greenlight.md#🙊 Great quotes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Greenlight: 🙊 Great quotes:\n> How you decide to act in the face of the inevitable decides your life. You can: Persist, Pivot, or Concede. It’s your choice, every time\n> One in a row. Any success only takes one. Then do it over and over\n> Change ‘I would if I could’ to ‘I can and I will’\n> But that road isn’t necessarily the road with the least traffic. It may be the road that we, personally, have traveled less.\n> I never wrote things down to remember; I always wrote things down so I could forget.\n> Sometimes which choice you make is not as important as making a choice and commiting to it.\n> It is not about win or lose, it is about do you accept the challenge","length":640,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"🙊 Great quotes","lines":[13,22]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-27: :\n⨑ MOOD::1\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::✅]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::✅]\n[Nutrients and Water::✅]","length":115,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md#\\Wednesday, March 27, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md#\\Wednesday, March 27, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-27.md#\\Wednesday, March 27, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-27: \\Wednesday, March 27, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n-\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [x] Review [[5. 🍕 Plans]] for 2024Q1\n\t- I think I am behind in some personal learning and side projects\n- [x] Review [[2024 Physical Goal Progression]]\n\t- Fighting to get into the routine of cross training for marathon, getting nutrition on track\n\t- Sleep quality is great\n\t- Marathon training is going\n- [ ] Work on [[Machine Learning Specialization]]\n- [x] Work on [[IG account of super obvious charts]]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n\n### Notes created today\n\n`