Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n-\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [ ]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n- [[GPT in SQL]]\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.cday = date(\"2024-03-12\") SORT file.ctime asc\n\n```\n\n### Notes last touched today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"2024-03-12\") SORT file.mtime asc\n\n```\n\ncreated: 2023-01-16 11:50","length":528,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Tuesday, March 12, 2024","lines":[9,50]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-06: :\n⨑ MOOD::\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::]\n[Nutrients and Water::]","length":111,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-06: \\Wednesday, March 06, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n- Played vball at Work\n- Played Zelda\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [ ]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n- #python #programming Dictionary vs Dataclasses\n\t- When choosing between a dictionary and dataclass structure in Python, there are a few considerations but one should remember that a dataclass is comprised of dictionaries under the hood\n\t1. Purpose - dictionary is a mapping type structure while a dataclass is a record type structure\n\t2. Function - dictionary is mean to be updated and mutable while dataclasses allow instances to be immutable upon creation, and prevents attributes from being removed or added. There are also better type hint support \n\t3. Efficiency - because dictionaries are simpler it is faster\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```datav","length":939,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024","lines":[9,45]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024#Notes last touched today":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024#Notes last touched today","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-06.md#\\Wednesday, March 06, 2024#Notes last touched today","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-06: \\Wednesday, March 06, 2024 > Notes last touched today:\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"2024-03-06\") SORT file.mtime asc\n\n```\n\ncreated: 2023-01-16 11:50","length":116,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Notes last touched today","lines":[46,55]},"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview: :\n---\nsource: []\nauthor:\n  - Paul Graham\ntags:\n  - \"#interview\"\n  - \"#industry\"\n---","length":81,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,7]},"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview: 📰 Summary (use your own words):\nWhat makes someone successful is that they are good at executing and persistence. And to be able to spot that is the key to the YC interview process.","length":150,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📰 Summary (use your own words)","lines":[8,10]},"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > What you can learn from how to ace a YC interview: ✍️ Notes:\n- Questions:\n\t- How to to ace your YC interview?\n\t- How it is mistaken to believe that the only way to become a billionaire is by exploiting people\n\t- And how these two things are interrelated\n- Paul's job is essentially a billionaire scout, and he claims after all these years, he still don't have one fast rule, and if he did have a rule it is definitely not seeing how well they exploit people\n\t- **YC looks for founders that can identify and make what a group of users wants**\n\t- YC's motto is \"make something people want\" #quotes\n\t- But in a market economy it is hard to define whether the \"want\" is real or not\n- ==The challenge is identifying if there is a path to a huge market==\n- A **larval market** is one that is small but growable\n\t- Apple when it was founded is an example of this\n\t\t- Not many people owned personal computers when Apple was founded in 1976, but today that market is massive\n\t- Fo","length":910,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[11,42]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Scaling Techniques.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Scaling Techniques.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Scaling Techniques.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Scaling Techniques: :\n#data-science #machine-learning \nImportant to note that we should not be leaking the testing data into scaling. We won't know the test data in practice so they shouldn't be used to inform us.\n\nStandard scaler\n- Removing the mean and dividing by the standard deviation\n- This changes the feature to be centered around 0 and has unit variance\n- Many ML models assumes this to be true for the features\nMin Max Scale\n- Removing the minimum and dividing by the range\n- This changes the feature to be between 0 and 1\nNormalizer\n- Scales each data point such that the feature vector has a Euclidean distance of 1\n- Used when the direction of the data matters but not the length of the vector","length":684,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,12]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Mixture of Models: :\n---\ntags:\n  - \"#machine-learning\"\n  - academic\nsource:\n  - https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.06538\n  - https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16668\n  - https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.03961\n  - https://blog.google/technology/ai/google-gemini-next-generation-model-february-2024/#architecture\n---","length":271,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,9]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#What is it?":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#What is it?","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#What is it?","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Mixture of Models: What is it?:\nA way to subdivide a giant neural network into sub-networks which can be expertly trained on one aspect of the problem and then sparsely combined back together for each task. \n\nThis means that during application, instead of using *all the parameters* of the model for any input the MoE model would select different parameters for each input. This also means that the MoE model has an outrageous number of parameters which are sparsely activated. \n\nThis idea was introduced in 1991 and while it made theoretical sense it was never practical because of several reasons:\n1. GPUs are faster at arithmetic than at branching\n2. Network bandwidth is a bottleneck between GPU clusters\n3. Large data sets are needed to train the multi-million to billion parameters\n4. The huge number of parameters is prone to training instability","length":821,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"What is it?","lines":[10,19]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#Architectures":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#Architectures","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mixture of Models.md#Architectures","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Mixture of Models: Architectures:\n- One architecture design is the \"sparsely-gated mixture-of-expert layer\". It uses the a sparse gating function to select two experts (two sub-networks) to perform the computation\n- Google developers introduced a module to scale the Transformer model with sparsely-gated MoE architecture back in 2006 with GShard\n- And then in 2022 introduced the Switch Transformer to simplify the MoE routing algorithm","length":404,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Architectures","lines":[20,24]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Mt Whitney.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mt Whitney.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Mt Whitney.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Mt Whitney: :\nSubmitted lottery\nDidn't win\nGoing to use the outdoor status to track for cancelled reservations","length":97,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,2]},"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > IG account of super obvious charts: :\nUsername: veryobviouscharts\nIdea: to create very obvious charts to illustrate a point\nWhy: to get better at chart generating code","length":130,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,3]},"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md#Ideas":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md#Ideas","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/IG account of super obvious charts.md#Ideas","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > IG account of super obvious charts: Ideas:\n- Movie poster characteristics (colors) and pie charts\n- Time series plot of character's known running balance of damage in a movie\n- Distance travelled in movies\n- Use AI to classify beard or no beard of current MLB players and show two bar plots \n- Gender pie chart of NBA\n\t- Done","length":282,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Ideas","lines":[4,10]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Euro Trip 2024.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Euro Trip 2024.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Euro Trip 2024.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Euro Trip 2024: :\n#travel \n\nWanderlog Link\n\nInteresting Things\n\nDavid\n- Is it before or after he has slain Goliath?\n- Michelangelo is known to free hand sculpting\n- The head is bigger proportion because it was intended to be on top of the Duomo building, so it would have looked correct if people viewed it from the ground\nColosseum","length":315,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,10]},"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > GPT in SQL: :\n---\nsource:\n  - https://explainextended.com/2023/12/31/happy-new-year-15/\nauthor:\n  - Quassnoi\ntags:\n  - machine-learning\n---","length":125,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,7]},"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > GPT in SQL: ✍️ Notes:\n- A generative LLM is a function that takes in some texts (prompt) and returns strings and numbers which represents the probability this string \"should\" be next \n\t- The function is **deterministic**\n\t- But how can GPTs give different answers? \n- The family of algorithms that found success in creating LLMs with good grammar and structure is called generative pre-trained transformers\n- The rough structure of a GPT is as follows\n```python\ndef generate(prompt: str) -> str:\n\ttokens = tokenize(prompt)\n\n\twhile True:\n\t\tcandidates = gpt2(tokens)\n\t\tnext_token = select_next_token(candidates)\n\n\t\ttokens.append(next_token)\n\n\t\tif should_stop_generating():\n\t\t\tbreak\n\n\tcomletion = detokenize(tokens)\n\treturn completion\n```\n\n### Tokenizer\n- converting from strings to numbers in an efficient way\n- byte-pair encoding was what was used in GPT2\n\t- start with encoding of 256 tokens, one for each byte\n\t- then find the most common 2-token combinations\n\t- assign","length":949,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[11,40]},"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Embedding":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Embedding","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Embedding","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > GPT in SQL: ✍️ Notes > Embedding:\n- Each token has orthogonal properties which can be encoded with vectors\n- The process of converting a token ID to a vector space is called embedding\n- How many properties should the vector space capture?\n\t- We don't know\n\t- Just need to create it large enough to capture *all* of it\n- GPT2 stores two kinds of embeddings as matrices: one for the tokens (WTE) and one for the position (WPE)\n\t- The values of the matrices are learned during training\n\t- The WPE shape limits the maximum number of tokens that can be used in a prompt for the GPT - which is 1024 in GPT2 and now up to 1million in Gemini 1.5 Pro","length":607,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Embedding","lines":[41,49]},"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Attention":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Attention","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/GPT in SQL.md#✍️ Notes#Attention","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > GPT in SQL: ✍️ Notes > Attention:\n- The groundbreaking feature of the transformer, it gives the ability to transfer some meaning from other token to the current one\n- Uses 12 sets of matrices called Q (query), K (key) and V (value)\n- Each matrix has 64 columns and together they form the attention matrix for each token\n\t- 3 x 12 x 64 columns for each token\n- Temperature\n\t- Changes how the softmax function selects the next token, can increase the \"creativeness\"","length":429,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Attention","lines":[50,56]},"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Apr 30 pull ups":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Apr 30 pull ups","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Apr 30 pull ups","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > 2024 Physical Goal Progression: 2024-Apr 30 pull ups:\n\n| Day            | **Reps** |  **Comment**   |\n| -------------- | -------- | :------------: |\n| [[2024-04-01]] | 11       | Starting point |\n| [[2024-04-03]] | 12       |                |\n| [[2024-04-04]] | 13       |                |\n| [[2024-04-08]] | 14       |                |\n| [[2024-04-18]] | 17       |                |\n| [[2024-04-23]] | 18       |                |\n| [[2024-04-29]] |          |                |","length":424,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"2024-Apr 30 pull ups","lines":[37,48]},"roam/5. 🍕 Plans.md#2024#Q2":{"key":"roam/5. 🍕 Plans.md#2024#Q2","path":"roam/5. 🍕 Plans.md#2024#Q2","embedding":{},"text":"roam > 5. 🍕 Plans: 2024 > Q2:\n- [ ] [[2024 Physical Goal Progression]]\n\t- [ ] 2 min dead hang\n\t- [ ] 1 arm pull up\n\t- [ ] 50 pull ups\n- [ ] Ramp up Marathon Training by getting more weekly volume\n- [ ] Find a hobby with hanh - drawing? reading? pottery? \n- [ ] Progress 50% in [[Machine learning specialization]]\n- [ ] Finish [[Crying in H Mart]]\n- [ ] Finish [[Misbelief]]\n- [ ] Finish [[]]\n- [ ] Plan hikes\n\t- Trans Catalina\n\t- [[Desolation Wilderness]] in June\n\t- [[Mt Whitney]] in Sept\n\t- Lost Coast\n\t- Waterfall somewhere\n- [ ] Practice sketching","length":521,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Q2","lines":[57,74]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-08: :\n⨑ MOOD::\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::✅]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::]\n[Nutrients and Water::✅]","length":113,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md#\\Monday, April 08, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md#\\Monday, April 08, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-08.md#\\Monday, April 08, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-08: \\Monday, April 08, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n-\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [ ]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n-\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.cday = date(\"2024-04-08\") SORT file.ctime asc\n\n```\n\n### Notes last touched today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"2024-04-08\") SORT file.mtime asc\n\n```\n\ncreated: 2023-01-16 11:50","length":513,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Monday, April 08, 2024","lines":[9,50]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-04: :\n⨑ MOOD::2\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::✅]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::]\n[Nutrients and Water::✅]","length":114,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-04: \\Thursday, April 04, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [ ]\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n- #til2024 XZ Backdoor\n\t- XZ utils is a utility that compress and decompressing streams that is included in many linux distros\n\t- There is a popular library that uses this and is maintained by a single maintainer called `liblzma` which some distros will patch their `sshd` library with \n\t\t- This contained a backdoor that can only be intercepted by a private key\n\t\t- The backdoor was obfuscated and hidden in the tarballs of the `liblzma` library as a test file\n\t- Discovered by a software engineer benchmarking postgres and accidentally saw that `ssh` was taking more resources than expected and tracked down this backdoor\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.cday","length":940,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Thursday, April 04, 2024","lines":[9,45]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024#Notes last touched today":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024#Notes last touched today","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-04.md#\\Thursday, April 04, 2024#Notes last touched today","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-04: \\Thursday, April 04, 2024 > Notes last touched today:\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"2024-04-04\") SORT file.mtime asc\n\n```\n\ncreated: 2023-01-16 11:50","length":116,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"Notes last touched today","lines":[46,55]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-28: :\n⨑ MOOD::2\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::]\n[Read::]\n[Personal Work::✅]\n[Nutrients and Water::]","length":113,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md#\\Thursday, March 28, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md#\\Thursday, March 28, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-03-28.md#\\Thursday, March 28, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-03-28: \\Thursday, March 28, 2024:\n\n### 📅 Daily Questions\n\n##### 🌜 Last night, after work, I\n\n- exercised, made food and planned for [[Euro Trip 2024]]\n\n##### 🙌 One thing I'm excited about right now is\n\n-\n\n##### 🚀 One+ thing I plan to accomplish today is\n\n- [x] Publish the chart on IG\n\t- FIRST POST DONE!\n\n##### 👎 One thing I'm struggling with today is\n\n-\n\n## 📝 Ephemeral Notes\n\n-\n\n### Notes created today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.cday = date(\"2024-03-28\") SORT file.ctime asc\n\n```\n\n### Notes last touched today\n\n```dataview\n\nList FROM \"\" WHERE file.mday = date(\"2024-03-28\") SORT file.mtime asc\n\n```\n\ncreated: 2023-01-16 11:50","length":613,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"\\Thursday, March 28, 2024","lines":[9,51]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?: :\n---\nsource:\n  - http://varianceexplained.org/r/bayesian-ab-testing/\nauthor:\n  - David Robinson\ntags:\n  - \"#statistics\"\n---","length":122,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,7]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?: 📰 Summary (use your own words):\nThe Bayesian AB testing approach does mitigate the problem of peeking but it *still increases the Type I error* which is not the promise that the Bayesian approach promises. Conversely, because the frequentist approach is promising a Type I error rate in the form of p-value testing, it is explicitly breaking that promise if we peeked **and** took action on the experiments.","length":377,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📰 Summary (use your own words)","lines":[8,10]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking?: ✍️ Notes:\n- We’ve concluded that **this advantage of Bayesian methods is overstated, or at least oversimplified.** Bayesian A/B testing is not “immune” to peeking and early-stopping. \n- Just like frequentist methods, peeking makes it more likely you’ll falsely stop a test. The Bayesian approach is, rather, _more careful than the frequentist approach about what promises it makes._\n- Bayesian method is less concerned about the null hypothesis testing but rather **posterior expected loss: the average amount we would lose by switching from A to B**\n\t- Expected loss is the combination of how probably that B has a less click through rate, and if B is worse, how much worse it is on average \n\t- If we peeked, we would still be increasing our Type I Error\n\t- However, Bayesian method doesn't make the promise of controlling for the Type I Error (unlike the frequentist approach)\n- If you plotted the two curves of peeking and no","length":918,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[11,23]},"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > How not to run an AB test: :\n---\nsource:\n  - https://www.evanmiller.org/how-not-to-run-an-ab-test.html\nauthor:\n  - Evan Miller\ntags:\n  - statistics\n---","length":122,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,7]},"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#📰 Summary (use your own words)","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > How not to run an AB test: 📰 Summary (use your own words):\n- Frequentist approach to statistical tests significance can be prone to large sample size","length":92,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"📰 Summary (use your own words)","lines":[8,10]},"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/How not to run an AB test.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > How not to run an AB test: ✍️ Notes:\n- Don't fall prey to \"repeated significance testing errors\"\n- When a A/B test says there is a \"95% of chance of beating the original\" or \"90% probability of statistical significance\"\n\t- It is asking the question \"what is the chance that we observe a difference like what we see in data randomly?\"\n\t- And that is the significance threshold (0.05 or 0.01)\n- However, this is precedent on a key decision -> **the sample size was fixed in advance**\n\t- Because might be tempted to take action *once we see significant results* and if that becomes insignificant later we wouldn't have known\n- So the key is to determine a sample size *before the experiement*\n\t- One rule of thumb is $N=16*\\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\delta^2}$ where $\\delta$ is the minimum effect you want to observe\n- For Bayesian A/B testing, this is less of a problem compared to the frequentist approach. But not completely fool-proof -> [[Is Bayesian AB testing immune to peeking","length":934,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[11,20]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Chasing Statistical Ghosts: :\n---\nsource:\n  - https://towardsdatascience.com/chasing-statistical-ghosts-in-experimentation-3f393323a1c1\nauthor:\n  - Colin McFarland\ntags:\n  - statistics\n  - machine-learning\n---","length":179,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md#✍️ Notes":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md#✍️ Notes","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/Chasing Statistical Ghosts.md#✍️ Notes","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > Chasing Statistical Ghosts: ✍️ Notes:\nFirst ghost - its either significant or noise\n- p value tries to answer the question - \"what is the likelihood that the observed differences due to the null hypothesis?\"\n\t- There is no such thing as \"the test is nearly significant\" or \"work towards significance\"\n\t- There is only significant or uncertain\n\nSecond ghost - the fallacy of session based metrics\n- If there are users creating multiple sessions, then it is difficult to assert the required assumption of all sessions are independent\n- This happens when you are measuring a rate metric which is defined by something other than what you randomized on, for example:\n\t- click through rate (total clicks / total impression) will be skewed if you are randomizing on users\n\nThird ghost - multiple comparisons\n- The more comparisons you make the hgiher your chances of seeing a false positive\n- The standard practice of 95% confidence level means that we expect a false positive r","length":933,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"✍️ Notes","lines":[12,38]},"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Mar One-arm pull up":{"key":"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Mar One-arm pull up","path":"slip-box/reference-notes/2024 Physical Goal Progression.md#2024-Mar One-arm pull up","embedding":{},"text":"slip-box > reference-notes > 2024 Physical Goal Progression: 2024-Mar One-arm pull up:\n| Day            | **Reps** |           **Comment**           |\n| -------------- | -------- | :-----------------------------: |\n| [[2024-04-01]] |          | Using bands to assist, 4 layers |\n| [[2024-04-03]] |          |                                 |\n| [[2024-04-23]] |          |                                 |","length":320,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":"2024-Mar One-arm pull up","lines":[30,36]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-18: :\n⨑ MOOD::\n\n↻ HABIT [[◶ Trackers]]\n[Sketching::]\n[Exercise::✅]\n[Read::✅]\n[Personal Work::]\n[Nutrients and Water::✅]","length":114,"class_name":"SmartBlock","heading":null,"lines":[0,8]},"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md#\\Thursday, April 18, 2024":{"key":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md#\\Thursday, April 18, 2024","path":"daily-notes/2024/2024-04-18.md#\\Thursday, April 18, 2024","embedding":{},"text":"daily-notes > 2024 > 2024-04-18: \\Thursda